First Author: Lehr
Title: Differential effects of donor factors on post-transplant survival in lung transplantation
Source number: 27  ￼

Very short summary: This SRTR study examined how donor factors and donor-recipient interactions affect post-transplant survival after bilateral lung transplantation. Five key findings were: (1) 7,609 adult primary bilateral lung transplant recipients were analyzed; (2) donor-factor effects varied by recipient age, sex, and diagnosis; (3) important interactions included donation after circulatory death in recipients aged 55–69 years, donor smoking in recipients aged 30–54 and ≥70 years, CMV mismatch in males, and donor diabetes in restrictive lung disease; (4) adding donor-factor interactions to the PT-CAS framework modestly improved 1-year discrimination from AUC 0.609 to 0.626; and (5) the combined Cox + PT-CAS model also improved calibration across a broader range of predicted risk. Tables 1–3, Figure 1, Figure 2, and Figure 3 were used directly for this extraction.  ￼

